WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.200
Every time Boston Dynamics puts out
one of their big YouTube productions,

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.640
the world is amazed.
The robots dance. Or they do parkour.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.800
All of which is very impressive, but
it's under controlled conditions in a lab.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.200
It's not exactly a
practical, real-world use.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.960
So what are those robots actually
being used for in the real world?

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.800
Inside this mountainside in western
Austria is a hydroelectric power plant.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.280
I would have filmed this piece-to-camera
actually down in the turbine hall, but...

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.120
[shouting over industrial noise]
It's a bit loud in here! 

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:30.880
At full capacity, 150 tons of water a second
goes down from the uphill reservoir,

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.480
through the turbines in the cavern,
generating 350 megawatts of power.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.520
And it's all run remotely.
Switching this plant on and off

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.600
is done at a control center
about half an hour's drive that way,

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.240
where they also control
twenty other hydro plants.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.440
On a normal day,
no-one works here at all.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.960
But for safety, someone does
have to regularly drive out to all those

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.560
twenty power plants, and check the
physical things are all as they should be.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.480
So you can probably see
where this is going.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.600
- We are using a robot to
continuously do inspection tasks,

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.880
and therefore testing if this is plausible
in the middle of the Alps.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.120
Energy Robotics is putting software
on robots to make them smart.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.800
We are a software company
and we're not building the hardware.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.960
We are writing code. 

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.120
Therefore, we ask other companies
to build the hardware for us,

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.280
or use existing robots already. 

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.440
We work with a lot of robot companies.
We're picking the best robot for the task. 

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.120
- Boston Dynamics and other companies build
the actual physical robots, and provide

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.280
a remote controller that someone can use
to directly move the bots around.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.200
But they also provide an interface
for computer code.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.760
The robot has built-in navigation
and obstacle avoidance,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.480
so in the same way that you can write code
to automate things

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.480
on a regular computer, you can write code
for the robot dog's computer too.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.280
Your code can learn the whole site
that you're working at

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.720
and tell the bot, OK,
find your way to this point.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.760
Look at this thing.
Take a picture or a temperature reading.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.240
And if that picture doesn't
look like it should,

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.520
or there's something blocking
your way, let us know about it.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.800
The robot is not remote controlled.
It's doing that task autonomously. 

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.840
That means we teach it a certain mission. 

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.640
And the robot does the
repetition of the mission.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.840
But it'll also be, in the future, able to
do something called "click and inspect".

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.000
That means we have a digital twin
of the environment,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.280
and then we're just saying
what the robot needs to inspect.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.960
And the robot plans a path towards the
inspection point and gets the information.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.440
The robot autonomously extracts
that information and reacts

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.360
based on the results,
which means either it sends an alarm

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.200
if it's out of the norm,
or it just collects information, then

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.480
puts it into the report
at the end of the mission. 

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:28.560
You can send robot dogs into toxic
or radioactive environments

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.960
humans shouldn't be in. 

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:33.440
But this isn't a particularly dangerous
site. They let me in, after all.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:34.920
It's just inconvenient to drive here, 

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.440
particularly in winter, when snowstorms
might close the mountain road entirely.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.080
This is a place designed
for humans, though,

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.760
which is why robots with wheels or tracks
aren't an option.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.920
- So it's important to have a robot which is
capable to operate in human environment,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.840
because all the infrastructure humans
have built has been built for humans.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.080
So therefore, we need the robot
to be capable of interacting

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.480
with its environment,
meaning simply: every other situation

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.680
like here where we need to climb
stairs, we're using a robot on legs.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.880
In the substation, 

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:05.480
where we have flat ground, for example,
we're using robots on wheels or on tracks.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.760
We're developing a skill
which we'll use in oil and gas,

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.080
where many of our robots
are actually working,

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.200
which we call "Man Down Detector",
so that if the robot detects someone lying

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.360
on the ground, because of whatever
incident, then the robot sounds an alarm,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.760
and therefore brings the troops
to help the person lying on the ground.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.840
- This trial will succeed or fail
based on two questions.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.880
First, does it work reliably? 

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.280
And second, if so, is it cheaper overall
than sending out people,

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.480
people, particularly when there might be
a shortage of engineers soon?

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.960
- The goal is to support human inspection,
and in the long run to replace certain parts,

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.040
especially those where humans need to be sent
in hazardous environments.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.240
In Germany, we have about
8,000 substations.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.480
All of those need to have inspections,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.760
and the number of substations is growing
due to the change to renewable energy.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.600
We need to have more robots on the ground,
because we have not enough

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.840
engineers in the workforce right now,
and we need about 400,000

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.120
more in the next ten years. 

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.360
The robot can perform up to twelve hours
of inspections: sixty minutes

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:06.360
walking around, sixty minutes charging,
so a lot of inspection every day.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.400
Over the next few years, 

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.280
we're also testing assisted manipulation,
where the robot is with a human

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.120
doing manipulation tasks
within infrastructure environments.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.360
In the future that might be
fully autonomous manipulation,

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.760
but there's probably some years to go.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.760
- If this works and if it gets rolled out,
then the power companies

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.120
will be saving employee time
and therefore money.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.880
In the same way that twenty power plants
can now be controlled from one center,

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.240
twenty power plants could be
inspected from one center,

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.480
 with actual human engineers

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.800
dispatched only when there's actually
something wrong that needs to be fixed.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.600
And I suspect it will be a while 

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.000
before the robots
start to take over that job as well.

